      "modern_form": "Proto-Iranian *raz- 'to leave, escape (one's notice)' (Cheung 2007: 322); Proto-Indo-Iranian *raȷ́ʰ- 'to abandon; secret'. No PIE root *redʰ-/*reǵʰ- 'to separate, isolate' is reconstructed by Pokorny, Walde-Pokorny, Watkins/AHD or Mann.",
      "note": "Nourai's root box is copied verbatim from his own source: Kent, Old Persian, p. 205b s.v. ¹rad- vb. 'leave': \"Skt. rah-, pres. rahati 'separates, leaves', to pIE *regh-, Av. razah- 'isolation', or to pIE *redh-, NPers. (through Ars. Phl.) rāhaδ 'he gets free from'\". Kent offers *regh- and *redh- as two MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE alternatives (the first if Av. razah- belongs here, the second if the New Persian verb does); Nourai merges them into a single root 'Redh, Regh' and blends the two glosses ('leave/abandon' from OP rad-, 'isolation' from Av. razah-) into 'to separate, isolate'. Modern handbooks do not accept either reconstruction: Pokorny has no such lemma (his p. 651 is lā-2 'to be concealed'), Watkins' AHD appendix and Mann's IEC have no *redh-/*reǵʰ- of this meaning, and Cheung (Etymological Dictionary of the Iranian Verb, s.v. *raz, p. 322 f.) concludes flatly \"No IE etymology can be cited for this Ir. root\" — Mayrhofer (EWAia II 442) likewise withdrew the older *legʷʰ- comparison of KEWA III 49. What IS solid is the Indo-Iranian level: Skt. rah 'to be lost, be lonely', rahas- n. 'secrecy, loneliness' = Ir. *raz, whence OP (ava-)rad-, MP/NP rastan/rah- and MP/NP rāz. So the family Nourai draws is real; the PIE label on it is not. The two branches of the chart are also not equally secure: the Avestan branch (node 3) is rejected by current scholarship.",
      "note_fa": "نورایی ریشه را «Redh, Regh» با معنی «جدا کردن، تنها کردن» و به استناد کنت (Kent, Old Persian, ص ۲۰۵) آورده است. کنت در واقع دو بازسازی را در برابر هم می‌گذارد: اگر razah- اوستایی هم‌ریشه باشد *regh- و اگر فعل فارسی نو در نظر باشد *redh-؛ نورایی این دو را در یک ریشه ادغام کرده و دو معنی را نیز در هم آمیخته است. هیچ‌یک از فرهنگ‌های امروزی هندواروپایی — پوکورنی، والده-پوکورنی، واتکینز (AHD) و مان — چنین ریشه‌ای را با این معنا نمی‌شناسند؛ چونگ در فرهنگ ریشه‌شناختی فعل ایرانی (ذیل *raz، ص ۳۲۲) صریحاً می‌نویسد که برای این ریشهٔ ایرانی هیچ ریشه‌شناسی هندواروپایی نمی‌توان آورد. آنچه استوار است سطح هندوایرانی است: ایرانی آغازین *raz «رها شدن، از نظر پنهان ماندن» برابر با سنسکریت rah- و rahas-، که فارسی باستان (ava-)rad-، فارسی میانه rastan/rah- و rāz از آن برمی‌آید. پس خانوادهٔ واژگانی نمودار درست است، اما برچسب «هندواروپایی» آن پذیرفتنی نیست و شاخهٔ اوستایی (گرهٔ ۳) را پژوهش امروزی رد می‌کند.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D8%B1%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%86",
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B2"
      ],
      "ref_check": [
        {
          "ref": "KNT:205",
          "status": "supports",
          "note": "Kent, Old Persian (KNT.txt, leaf 230 = printed pp. 205-206), s.v. ¹rad-: \"¹rad- vb. 'leave': Skt. rah-, pres. rahati 'separates, leaves', to pIE *regh-, Av. razah- 'isolation', or to pIE *redh-, NPers. (through Ars. Phl.) rāhaδ 'he gets free from'; Fr. Müller, WZKM 11.203-4; Foy, KZ 37.564-5; Bthl. AiW 1505; MB Gr. §192.\" This is verbatim the source of Nourai's root name, of both branch languages and of the reference BRT:1505 he puts on node 2. Kent's grammar section (§ on injunctives) repeats \"pIE *reghe- (or *redhe-, see Lex. s.v.), Skt. rahati 'he leaves'; inj. 2d sg. OP ava-rada\". Note that Kent presents the two roots as alternatives, not as one root."
        }
      ],
      "consulted": [
        {
          "src": "POK",
          "where": "POK_full.txt, lemma list 'Root / lemma: re…' and p. 651",
          "stance": "silent",
          "note": "Pokorny has red-, red-1, red-2, reg-1, reg-2, reidʰ-, rendʰ- etc., but no *redʰ-/*reǵʰ- 'to separate, isolate'; 'rahati' occurs nowhere in the IEW text (grep 'rahati' hits only Czech z-drahati se). Pokorny p. 651, the page Cheung cites for Skt. rah, is 'lā-2 (*leh₂k-) Meaning: to be concealed, covered' (Gk. λανθάνω, Lat. lateō) — a different, semantically 'hidden' etymology, and even there no Indo-Iranian form is listed."
        },
        {
          "src": "WLD",
          "where": "WLD_Bd1_pages.txt / WLD_Bd2_pages.txt (grep 'rahati', 'razah')",
          "stance": "silent",
          "note": "Walde-Pokorny nowhere quotes Skt. rahati or Av. razah-; the only 'drahati' hit is Czech z-drahati se under a wholly different root. No *redʰ- 'separate' lemma."
        },
        {
          "src": "AHD",
          "where": "AHD_watkins1985.txt (grep '^redh', '^regh', 'reĝh')",
          "stance": "silent",
          "note": "Watkins' Indo-European Roots appendix has no entry *redh- or *regh- of this meaning (only ghredh- 'to walk, go', reg- 'to move in a straight line', etc.). Nourai's root has no counterpart in AHD."
        },
        {
          "src": "IEC",
          "where": "IEC.txt (grep 'redh-', 'reĝh-', 'rahati')",
          "stance": "silent",
          "note": "Mann's Indo-European Comparative Dictionary has no *redh-/*reĝh- 'separate, isolate' lemma; the only -redh- entries are bhrodhos 'ford' and ueredh- 'seethe, rile'."
        },
        {
          "src": "CHEUNG",
          "where": "CHEUNG.txt pp. 322-323, s.v. *raz 'to leave, escape (one's notice)'",
          "stance": "partial",
          "note": "Cheung collects exactly Nourai's family — OP (+*aua-) avarad-, MMP rh-/BMP lh- /rah-/, BMP lstn /rastan/, NP rastan/rah-, NP rāz, Sogd. r'z, Khot. rrays(s)aa- 'empty', Skt. rah, rahas- — under Ir. *raz, and adds \"The meanings 'secret' and 'to flee, escape' are not necessarily incompatible, if we assume an older meaning 'to escape (one's notice)'\". But on the IE level: \"The IE etymology cited in his previous work KEWA III: 49 (IE *leg\"- 'to lay'…) is doubted by Mayrhofer in EWAia\" and \"No IE etymology can be cited for this Ir. root\" (the latter under his separate root *(h)rah). So Cheung supports the Indo-Iranian grouping but not Nourai's PIE root."
        },
